exports[`--help snapshots > test plan 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite test plan [options] [command]

Generate + accept AI test plans (V3), and manage FE plan-steps

Options:
  -h, --help               display help for command

Commands:
  put [options] <test-id>  Replace an FE test's planSteps[] (BE tests return
                           400 → use 'test code put')
  generate [options]       Generate AI test-case proposals for a project — runs
                           only the missing pipeline stages and stages the
                           results server-side for review (nothing is written
                           locally)
  accept [options]         Convert staged proposals into real test cases (free)
                           — all of them, or a subset with --only
  help [command]           display help for command
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > test plan accept 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite test plan accept [options]

Convert staged proposals into real test cases (free) — all of them, or a subset
with --only

Options:
  --project <id>             project id (V3-native; V2 ids resolve through the
                             migration bridge)
  --only <ids...>            accept only these proposal ids (space- or
                             comma-separated; the ids come from the \`test plan
                             generate\` table). Accepting a subset discards the
                             rest. ANY id that matches no staged proposal is a
                             validation error — the request is never sent.
  --idempotency-key <token>  opaque idempotency token (1-256 ASCII chars).
                             Defaults to a UUIDv4 minted per invocation; pin
                             one yourself for safe retries.
  -h, --help                 display help for command

Notes:
  - Accepting adds no charge of its own; generation already did the work.
  - API test code is generated when the tests first run, not at accept.
  - Accepting a subset discards the remaining staged proposals (the staging
    area is cleared either way) — the output says how many were discarded.
  - Nothing staged → exit 6 with a pointer at \`test plan generate\`.


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > test plan generate 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite test plan generate [options]

Generate AI test-case proposals for a project — runs only the missing pipeline
stages and stages the results server-side for review (nothing is written
locally)

Options:
  --project <id>             project id (V3-native; V2 ids resolve through the
                             migration bridge)
  --timeout <seconds>        total wait budget in seconds (1-3600, default
                             1800; exploration takes minutes on a fresh
                             frontend project)
  --idempotency-key <token>  opaque idempotency token (1-256 ASCII chars).
                             Defaults to a UUIDv4 minted per invocation; pin
                             one yourself for safe retries.
  -h, --help                 display help for command

Behavior:
  - Runs the stages the project still needs (exploration for frontend,
    strategy, proposals) and stops when proposals are staged for review.
    Nothing is written to disk.
  - Interrupt (exit 130/143/129) or timeout (exit 7): the stage in
    progress finishes on its own, the rest wait. Run the same command
    again to continue; it picks up where it left off. A stage that
    already completed is never charged again.
  - Workspace credits are spent per stage that runs; the result line reports
    what THIS run charged (omitted when nothing new was charged).
    \`testsprite usage\` shows your balance.

Next step: review the printed table, then \`testsprite test plan accept\`.


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > test rerun 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite test rerun [options] [test-ids...]

Re-execute a test (or multiple) as a replay — FE replays the saved script, BE re-runs the dependency closure. Billed the same as a fresh run: 0.5 credits per FE rerun / 0.2 credits per BE rerun (legacy V2 accounts: FE rerun remains free).

Exit codes:
  0  passed (or queued without --wait)
  1  failed / blocked / cancelled
  3  auth error
  4  test not found (single), or batch: nothing queued — every id had no replayable run
  5  validation error, or --all resolved zero tests (pass --allow-empty to exit 0)
  6  conflict (already running — see nextAction for the active runId)
  7  timeout or deferred — resume with: testsprite test wait <run-id>, or stop it with: testsprite test cancel <run-id>
 11  rate limited — honor Retry-After

On failure/blocked/cancelled, run: testsprite test artifact get <run-id>

Ctrl-C during --wait detaches only (the run keeps executing and billing);
stop it for real with: testsprite test cancel <run-id>

Options:
  --all                       rerun all tests in the resolved project (requires
                              --project) (default: false)
  --project <id>              project id (required with --all; returned by
                              \`testsprite project list\`)
  --skip-terminal             with --all: skip tests already in a terminal
                              status (passed|failed|blocked|cancelled)
                              (default: false)
  --status <list>             with --all: only dispatch tests whose status
                              matches one of these values (comma-separated;
                              accepted:
                              draft|ready|queued|running|passed|failed|blocked|cancelled|unknown)
  --filter <substr>           with --all: only rerun tests whose name contains
                              this substring (case-insensitive)
  --wait                      block until terminal status or --timeout elapses
                              (default: false)
  --no-wait                   detach without opening an automatic environment
                              tunnel
  --skip-preflight            skip the loopback port probe before opening a
                              tunnel
  --timeout <s>               with --wait, max seconds to wait (1–3600, default
                              600)
  --no-auto-heal              replay the saved script verbatim instead of
                              letting the agent repair it (default: auto-heal
                              is ON for FE reruns). When heal engages, the
                              agent re-authors the test and the new code
                              replaces the stored version. Ignored for backend
                              reruns. Costs: \`testsprite usage\`.
  --skip-dependencies         BE only: rerun only the named test without
                              expanding the producer/teardown closure (default:
                              false)
  --env <name>                replay against the named project environment —
                              its test-account credentials, auto-auth and OTP
                              settings (names: \`testsprite project env list
                              <project-id>\`). Omit to keep using the project's
                              default environment. Loopback URLs open a tunnel
                              automatically. Applies to every test of a batch
                              rerun.
  --max-concurrency <n>       with a tunnel, max runs in flight (1-10, default
                              5); otherwise with --wait, max polls (1-100,
                              default 50)
  --idempotency-key <key>     opaque key for safe retries (1–256 chars).
                              Printed to stderr at --verbose if auto-generated.
  --report <format>           with batch --wait: write a JUnit XML sidecar
                              report after polling (accepted: junit)
  --report-file <path>        output path for --report (atomic write)
  --report-suite-name <name>  optional JUnit <testsuite name=...> override
                              (default: testsprite:<projectId>)
  --gh-output                 with batch --wait: emit GitHub-native output
                              (::error:: annotations for failed/timed-out runs,
                              ::warning:: for never-dispatched tests;
                              job-summary table when $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY is
                              set). Auto-enabled when GITHUB_ACTIONS=true
  --summary-file <path>       with batch --wait: also write the reduced machine
                              summary JSON {total, passed, failed, skipped,
                              timedOut, runs[]} to this file
  --allow-empty               with --all: exit 0 when the resolved test set is
                              empty (no tests match
                              --filter/--status/--skip-terminal, or the project
                              has none). Default: fail with exit 5 — a
                              zero-dispatch rerun is a CI false-green
  -h, --help                  display help for command

Notes:
  • Frontend reruns default to auto-heal; the server controls the default for a
    fresh \`test run\`. Use --no-auto-heal to request a strict replay where supported.
  • What still differs: rerun replays a SAVED run/script, so one has to exist;
    \`test run\` starts fresh. A BE rerun also expands the dependency closure.
  • Under --wait the per-request HTTP timeout is auto-raised to cover --timeout so a
    slow trigger/poll under load is not cut at the 120s default (see --request-timeout).
  • Batch --wait: rate-deferred tests appear in \`deferred[]\` and \`summary.deferred\`,
    and force a non-zero exit — they are NOT counted in \`summary.total\` (dispatched only).
  • rerun gets no server echo of the effective auto-heal decision, so a dropped
    opt-out cannot be detected here the way \`test run --no-auto-heal\` detects it.

Dry-run shape notes:
  • --dry-run shows the BE rerun wire shape (includes \`closure{}\`); FE rerun responses
    omit \`closure\` (or return it as null) since there is no dependency expansion.
  • \`autoHeal\` defaults true for FE reruns; BE reruns ignore the field entirely.

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > test result 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite test result [options] <test-id>

Get the latest result for a test (default) or list prior runs (--history).

--output json shape differs by mode:
  (default)  single CliLatestResult object
  --history  { runs: RunHistoryItem[], nextCursor: string|null, meta: RunHistoryMeta }

Per-run detail: testsprite test wait <run-id>
Failure bundle:  testsprite test artifact get <run-id>

Options:
  --include-analysis  attach the inline \`analysis\` block (rootCauseHypothesis,
                      recommendedFixTarget, failureKind, snapshotId) (default:
                      false)
  --history           list prior runs for this test instead of showing the
                      latest result
  --source <src>      with --history: filter by trigger source
                      (cli|portal|mcp|schedule|github_action)
  --since <dur>       with --history: lower bound on createdAt — 24h, 7d, or
                      ISO timestamp (client-side translated)
  --page-size <n>     with --history: number of runs per page (1–100, default
                      20)
  --cursor <token>    with --history: opaque cursor from a prior page
  --rerun             with --history: show only reruns
  --no-rerun          with --history: show only fresh (non-rerun) runs
  --env <name>        with --history: only runs whose credentials came from
                      this project environment
  --columns <list>    with --history: select/reorder text table columns
  --no-header         with --history: suppress the text table header row
  -h, --help          display help for command

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > test run 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite test run [options] [test-id...]

Trigger one or several test runs. Saved loopback environments open a tunnel automatically and wait for a verdict.
Use --all --project <id> for a wave-ordered batch run. Loopback environments share one tunnel.

Exit codes:
  0  passed (or queued without --wait)
  1  failed / blocked / cancelled
  3  auth error
  4  test not found
  5  validation error (e.g., bad URL, or positional + --all both set)
  6  conflict (already running — see nextAction for the active runId)
  7  timeout — resume with: testsprite test wait <run-id>, or stop it with: testsprite test cancel <run-id>
 10  transport/network failure (UNAVAILABLE) — retry the command
 11  rate limited — honor Retry-After, or the in-flight run cap was hit
     (details.reason "inflight_cap"; see: testsprite test run --help)

On failure/blocked/cancelled, run: testsprite test artifact get <run-id>

Ctrl-C during an ordinary --wait detaches; an owned tunnel cancels unfinished runs by default.
Stop a detached run with: testsprite test cancel <run-id>
Local environments: dev servers can take a minute per page through the tunnel.
For a heavy dev server, test a built server: npm run build, then your framework preview/start command.

Options:
  --target-url <url>          frontend tests only: use the environment already
                              pointing at this URL's origin, or a temporary one
                              TestSprite deletes when the run finishes; with
                              --env, use its settings against this URL;
                              loopback URLs open a tunnel automatically
  --skip-preflight            skip the pre-charge reachability probe for a
                              target URL. The probe runs from this machine, not
                              from the Lambda that executes the test, so it can
                              occasionally be wrong (e.g. an IP allowlist that
                              permits the Lambda but not this machine); use
                              this flag when you know better. Zero network
                              calls when set. (default: false)
  --local <port>              run against an app on THIS machine, reached
                              through a TestSprite tunnel. The test runner
                              navigates to http://localhost:<port> and its
                              traffic is proxied back to you for as long as
                              this command runs. Implies --wait (the tunnel
                              closes when the command exits, so a detached run
                              could not finish). Frontend tests only; a
                              matching saved environment keeps its host
                              spelling; several ids or --all share one tunnel
                              (5 concurrent by default, at most 10). Before
                              anything is minted or billed, the port is probed
                              and a dead one is refused.
  --local-host <host>         with --local, override which loopback address to
                              name in the run's target URL — one of localhost,
                              127.0.0.1, ::1 (default: saved matching host,
                              else localhost). Use localhost if your app only
                              answers on that name, or ::1 for an IPv6-only
                              listener.
  --tunnel-client <id>        for a loopback environment or --local, attach to
                              a tunnel already running under \`testsprite tunnel
                              start\` instead of opening a new one. The id is
                              the non-secret handle that command prints; the
                              tunnel stays owned by that process and is not
                              closed when this run or batch finishes.
  --no-cancel-on-interrupt    with a tunnel, skip automatic cancellation when
                              an owned tunnel is about to close or a borrowed
                              tunnel's owner disappears. An ordinary interrupt
                              of a borrowed run never cancels it because its
                              tunnel remains alive.
  --env <name>                run against the named project environment — its
                              test-account credentials, auto-auth and OTP
                              settings (names: \`testsprite project env list
                              <project-id>\`). A loopback environment opens a
                              tunnel automatically. The run opens that
                              environment's URL. Omit --env to keep using the
                              project's default environment. Also applies with
                              --all.
  --wait                      poll until terminal status or --timeout elapses
                              (default: false)
  --no-wait                   detach without opening an automatic environment
                              tunnel
  --timeout <s>               with --wait, max seconds per run (1–3600; default
                              600, or 1200 through a tunnel)
  --idempotency-key <key>     opaque key for safe retries (1–256 chars); local
                              batches append each test id. Auto-generated keys
                              print to stderr in JSON, verbose or debug mode.
  --all                       run all tests in the project (wave-ordered fresh
                              run; uses --project or TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID).
                              Mutually exclusive with <test-id>. (default:
                              false)
  --project <id>              project id (with --all, overrides
                              TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID; returned by \`testsprite
                              project list\`)
  --filter <substr>           with --all: only run tests whose name contains
                              this substring (case-insensitive)
  --max-concurrency <n>       with a tunnel, max runs in flight through one
                              tunnel (1-10, default 5); with --all --wait
                              without a tunnel, max polls (1-100, default 50)
  --report <format>           with a --wait batch (--all or several ids): write
                              a JUnit XML sidecar report after polling
                              (accepted: junit)
  --report-file <path>        output path for --report (atomic write)
  --report-suite-name <name>  optional JUnit <testsuite name=...> override
                              (default: testsprite:<projectId>)
  --gh-output                 with --wait (single test or batch): emit
                              GitHub-native output (::error:: annotations for
                              failed/timed-out runs, ::warning:: for
                              never-dispatched tests; job-summary table when
                              $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY is set). Auto-enabled when
                              GITHUB_ACTIONS=true
  --summary-file <path>       with --wait (single test or batch): also write
                              the reduced machine summary JSON {total, passed,
                              failed, skipped, timedOut, runs[]} to this file
  --allow-empty               with --all: exit 0 when the run dispatches ZERO
                              tests (all skipped / empty project / --filter
                              matched nothing). Default: fail with exit 5 — a
                              zero-dispatch run is a CI false-green
  --no-auto-heal              ask the server to replay stored test code instead
                              of letting the agent heal it. The server controls
                              whether fresh runs heal drifted generated code by
                              default; when healing is enabled, the agent
                              re-authors stale code rather than replaying a
                              script that can only fail. Use this flag to
                              request a strict replay for a regression gate or
                              a reproduction you are bisecting. Code you wrote
                              yourself is never re-authored. Where stored-code
                              replay is unavailable, the server rejects this
                              flag. Where replay is available but a test has no
                              replayable stored code, the server may refuse the
                              run instead of authoring it. An older server may
                              ignore the flag; the CLI prints an [advisory]
                              when the server reports that the opt-out did not
                              take.
  -h, --help                  display help for command

Dependency-aware fresh run:
  testsprite test run --all --project <id>                run all project tests in wave order
  TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID=<id> testsprite test run --all    use env default project
  testsprite test run <id> <id> --local 3000             several local tests, one tunnel
  testsprite test run --all --project <id> --local 3000  all frontend tests, one tunnel
  testsprite test run <id> <id> --local 3000 --max-concurrency 3
  testsprite test run --all --filter <substr>             name-glob subset (uses --project/env)
  testsprite test run --all --wait --report junit --report-file ./results.xml
  project id precedence: --project wins over TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID

BE tests can declare --produces/--needs at create time to drive wave ordering
(see \`testsprite test create --help\` for details).

Frontend tests: the current unified engine runs FE tests too (they are billed
like any run). On the legacy backend-only engine FE tests cannot run — they are
reported under skippedFrontend with an advisory; run those with 'test run <id>'.

Concurrency limits:
  - CLI run triggers are refused once 50 of your runs are already in
    flight (server default; the refusal reports your real cap). Runs from every
    source count toward it (CLI, portal, schedules, CI), but only CLI triggers
    are refused on it.
    At the cap this command accepts nothing and returns every id deferred,
    which exits 7; resume with the printed ids once runs finish. (A single
    \`test run <id>\` over the cap exits 11 with details.reason "inflight_cap",
    and is not auto-retried — runs have to finish first.)
    Poll what is already running with: testsprite test wait <run-id...>
  - Separately, run triggers are throttled per minute. That one does clear on
    its own and is retried for you.
  - --max-concurrency here bounds the --wait poll fan-out, not dispatch, so it
    has no effect on the cap. A smaller batch does.

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

