exports[`--help snapshots > top 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite [options] [command]

Official TestSprite command-line interface

Options:
  -V, --version                output the version number
  --output <mode>              Output format (json|text) (default: "text")
  --profile <name>             Configuration profile to use
  --endpoint-url <url>         Override the API endpoint host
  --verbose                    Emit human-readable HTTP retry / backoff /
                               polling-mode transitions to stderr. Less noisy
                               than --debug; useful for diagnosing hangs
                               without the full trace.
  --debug                      Print HTTP method/path, request id, latency,
                               retry decisions to stderr
  --dry-run                    Skip the network and credentials; emit a canned
                               sample matching the OpenAPI contract. Useful for
                               learning the CLI surface without an API key.
                               Note: file inputs you pass
                               (--plan-from/--plans/--steps) are still read and
                               validated locally; only --code-file uses a
                               placeholder.
  --request-timeout <seconds>  Client-side per-request timeout in seconds
                               (default: 120). Aborts any single fetch that
                               does not complete within this deadline. Override
                               via TESTSPRITE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS env var
                               (milliseconds). Range: 1–600. Does not affect
                               the --timeout polling ceiling for \`test
                               run/wait\`.
  -h, --help                   display help for command

Commands:
  setup [options]              Set up TestSprite: configure your API key and
                               install the TestSprite agent skills for your
                               coding agent
  auth                         Manage TestSprite credentials
  project                      Manage TestSprite projects
  test                         Inspect TestSprite tests
  testlist                     Manage test lists (groups of tests)
  ci                           Scaffold CI integration for TestSprite
  schedule                     Manage TestSprite schedules
  tunnel                       Open a tunnel so TestSprite can reach an app on
                               this machine
  agent                        Install TestSprite guidance into coding-agent
                               config (Claude Code, Cursor, Cline, Antigravity,
                               Kiro, Windsurf, Copilot, Codex)
  usage|credits                Show credit balance and plan/entitlement info
                               (proactive pre-flight before a large test run)
  doctor                       Diagnose CLI setup: version, Node, profile,
                               endpoint, credentials, connectivity, skill
  completion [shell]           Print a shell completion script (bash|zsh|fish)
  help [command]               display help for command
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > tunnel 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite tunnel [options] [command]

Open a tunnel so TestSprite can reach an app on this machine

Options:
  -h, --help                  display help for command

Commands:
  start [options]             Open a tunnel and hold it until you stop the
                              command (Ctrl-C)
  list                        List live tunnel bindings for this account
  status <client-id>          Report whether a tunnel client of yours is
                              connected
  stop [options] [client-id]  Destroy one tunnel credential or stop every live
                              tunnel
  help [command]              display help for command

Frontend runs automatically open and close a tunnel when the selected environment URL
is on this machine. Use these commands when you want one tunnel to serve several runs, or to check on
one that a run reported as down.

A tunnel lives only as long as \`tunnel start\` is running — there is no background
daemon, because the credential that authorises an inbound network path into this machine
is never written to disk.

Examples:
  testsprite tunnel start                                   # hold a tunnel open (Ctrl-C to stop)
  testsprite test run <id> --env <name> --tunnel-client <id>
  testsprite tunnel list
  testsprite tunnel status <id>
  testsprite tunnel stop <id>
  testsprite tunnel stop --all --confirm

"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > tunnel list 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite tunnel list [options]

List live tunnel bindings for this account

Options:
  -h, --help  display help for command

Shows each tunnel client id, connection status, creation time and expiry.
Includes tunnels automatically opened for environments on this machine.

Exit codes:
  0  listed (possibly empty)
  3  auth error — the key needs the \`run:tunnel\` scope
  4  server too old to list tunnels
 10  could not reach TestSprite


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > tunnel start 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite tunnel start [options]

Open a tunnel and hold it until you stop the command (Ctrl-C)

Options:
  --ttl <seconds>  requested lifetime of the tunnel credential (60–28800). The
                   server clamps this, and the credential is deleted when the
                   command exits regardless — the lifetime only matters if this
                   process is killed without cleaning up.
  -h, --help       display help for command

Prints the client id to stderr as soon as it is minted, before connecting; pass it
to \`test run <id> --env <name> --tunnel-client <id>\` for an environment on this machine.
Frontend runs normally open their own tunnel automatically for a loopback environment URL.

Dev servers can take a minute per page through the tunnel. For a heavy dev server,
test a built server: \`npm run build\`, then your framework's preview/start command.

Exit codes:
  0  you stopped it (Ctrl-C is the normal way to end this command)
  3  auth error — the key needs the \`run:tunnel\` scope; mint a new key
 10  the tunnel service disconnected the client, or it never connected
 11  rate limited, or too many tunnels are already open for this account


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > tunnel status 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite tunnel status [options] <client-id>

Report whether a tunnel client of yours is connected

Options:
  -h, --help  display help for command

\`offline\` means the API answered and said nothing is connected with that id. A failure
to reach TestSprite is reported as its own error, never as \`offline\`.
Use the client id printed by a run that automatically opened a tunnel.

Exit codes:
  0  answered (the answer may be \`offline\`)
  4  no such tunnel for this account
 10  could not reach TestSprite to ask


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > tunnel stop 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite tunnel stop [options] [client-id]

Destroy one tunnel credential or stop every live tunnel

Options:
  --all       stop every live tunnel on this account (default: false)
  --confirm   required with --all for this destructive operation (default:
              false)
  -h, --help  display help for command

Stopping one that is already gone succeeds. Revoking the credential
also makes a running \`tunnel start\` exit within ~15 s.
A run that automatically opened this tunnel loses access to its environment when you stop it.
Stopping all requires --confirm; --dry-run previews sample ids without it.

Exit codes:
  0  stopped (or already absent), including an empty --all result
  1  at least one tunnel could not be stopped
  3  auth error — the key needs the \`run:tunnel\` scope
  4  server too old to list tunnels (with --all)
  5  invalid arguments or --all without --confirm
 10  could not reach TestSprite


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;
